 *OOP Concept Demonstrated* 

The program uses runtime polymorphism through the abstract ""Account"" class. The ""calculateInterest()"", ""display()"", and ""withdraw()"" functions can be overridden by derived account classes, allowing each account type to implement its own behavior.

 *Objective* 

To understand how abstract classes, inheritance, polymorphism, function overriding, and smart pointers can be used to design a simple banking system supporting multiple types of accounts.

 *Key Concepts Demonstrated* 

- Abstraction – Defines common banking operations using the abstract "Account" class.
- Inheritance – "SavingsAccount", "CurrentAccount", and "FixedDepositAccount" inherit from "Account".
- Polymorphism – Different account types implement interest calculation and display differently.
- Function Overriding – Derived classes override "calculateInterest()", "display()", and "withdraw()" where required.
- Encapsulation – Account data is maintained within classes.
- Pure Virtual Function – "calculateInterest()" defines the required interest calculation interface.
- Smart Pointers – "unique_ptr" provides automatic memory management.
- Vector – Stores multiple account objects in a single collection.
- Constructors – Initialize account details and interest-related information.
- Virtual Destructor – Ensures proper destruction of derived account objects.
